BD-J: do not depend on a security manager to materialise BD-ROM files

When the disc is read through libudfread (an image file or a raw device, i.e.
whenever the player does not hand libbluray a plain directory), BD-ROM files
are not reachable with standard Java I/O. VFSCache copies them into its cache
on demand -- but the only thing that ever triggers that copy is
BDJSecurityManager.checkPermission(), and the only thing that calls *that* is
the security manager check inside java.io.FileInputStream:

    SecurityManager security = System.getSecurityManager();
    if (security != null) {
        security.checkRead(name);      /* -> BDJLoader.accessFile() */
    }

Java 17 disabled the security manager by default and Java 18 made installing
one throw; libbluray works around that by reflecting on
System.implSetSecurityManager(). Measured on Java 21 (JBR 21.0.7) that
workaround does not take: getSecurityManager() still answers null on every
single file open -- 20 out of 20 in a BD-J title. So accessFile() is never
called, nothing is ever fetched from the disc, and every read of a BD-ROM file
that was not explicitly listed in the BDJO application cache fails.

That is not a corner case. Discs routinely keep their resources in a directory
next to the .jar rather than inside it, and only the .jar is declared in the
cache info. Measured on Rio (org 0x7fff646c): BDMV/JAR/01001/ holds 21 loose
PNGs, the menu Xlet loads seven of them by absolute VFS path, all seven fail,
and sun.awt.image.FileImageSource reports imageComplete(IMAGEERROR) for each.
The Xlet then draws those empty images successfully, so the root window's back
buffer stays all-zero, libbluray withholds the overlay ("sync() ignored
(overlay not open, empty overlay)") and the disc shows no menu at all -- while
BD-J itself, the Xlets and the menu's background playlist all run correctly.
The same failure explains the FileNotFoundExceptions the disc's own code logs
for BDMV/JAR/89000/preroll.properties and CERTIFICATE/id.bdmv.

Call accessFile() from the open path itself. It checks the cache before doing
any work, guards against re-entry, and returns immediately when the disc is a
directory (cacheAll false), so this costs nothing where things already worked
and does not change behaviour when a security manager *is* active -- the file
is simply already cached by the time checkRead() would have asked for it.

